OSI model and TCP/IP model

OSI (Open System Interconnection) model - demonstrate the theory behind networking
TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol) model - in practical, the real-world networking is based-off.

OSI layers
1. Application layer:
  - provides an interface to application to transmit over network
  - data passed down to the presentation layer
2. Presentation layer:
  - convert the data into a standardised format
  - encryption/decryption, compression, or other data transformation will be handled
3. Session layer:
  - setup connection with computer over the network
  - connection establishment and maintenance
  - the connection established will be unique (thus, multiple connetion simultaneously is possible)
4. Transport layer:
  - select the protocol to be used (TCP or UDP)
  - data converted to bite-sized pieces (TCP - segments, UDP - datagrams)
5. Network layer:
  - locating the destination requested
  - logical addressing (provide order to network, categorizing, and sorting them) - IPv4 amd IPv6 formating
  - also best route for transmission will be determined
6. Data link layer:
  - physical addressing - MAC (Media Access Control) address 
  - helps to find out the exact system
  - NIC (Network Interface Card) imprints the MAC address, which will be done by the manufacturer
  - where it comes to receiving data it does the error checking to make sure that the data is not corrupted during the transmission (checksum).
7. Physical layer:
  - right down to the hardware
  - converts binary data into digital signals for transmission and vice versa, while receiving

Encapsulation - the process of adding specific information to the data as it passes through the layers of the models
- Application layer header added -> Data
- Presentation layer header added -> Data
- Session layer header added -> Data
- Transport layer header added -> Data (Segments or datagrams)
- Network layer header added -> Data (packets)
- Data link layer header and trailer added -> Data (frames)
- Data sent across the network -> Data stream (bits)
De-encapsulation - the reverse process of the above while receiving the data; detaching the additional info that was added in each layers.

TCP / IP model: Similar to the OSI model; basis of real-world networking.
1. Application
2. Transport
3. Internet
4. Network interface
Sometimes the network layer will be divided into data link layer and physical layer which makes it 5 layers; also valid.
Basically, there are suits of protocols in these models.
TCP - controls the flow of data between two endpoints
UDP - controls how packets are addressed and sent

TCP -> connection-based protocol (lossless protocol)
3-way handshake
1. Client to server -> SYN (synchronise)
2. Server to client -> SYN/ACK 
3. Client to server -> ACK (Acknowledge)

Network tools:
1. PING
  - whether a connection to a remote server is possible or not. It works using ICMP protocol
  - It can also be used for determining IP address of server hosting the website
  - on website and computer on your network
2. TRACEROUTE
  - follow-up of ping, to understand the path that the requests takes to reach destination
3. WHOIS 
  - helps to get details about domain names, to whom it is registered to, etc.
  DNS (Domain Name System) - maps the ip address with names of webpages
4. Dig 
  - manually requesting domain names to DNS servers, helps in network troubleshooting
DNS:
When we request for a website, it checks for the ip - domain mapping in the following order
1. Host files (device)
2. local dns cache (device)
3. recursive DNS server
4. root name DNS server
5. top-level domain (tld) server [.com, .in, etc]
6. authoritative name server

TTL (Time to live) - tells the systen till when the record is valid or when it should request again for the record. It will be measured in seconds.
